<Base title="Sources and methodology" description="How TheTravelFact chooses a station, a source, and a date, and what was left off.">
  <article class="shell page prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How a number gets on the page</h1>
    <p>The first twenty sheets were checked 14 November 2026. Milan through Japan, Tokyo through Croatia, Dubrovnik through Chile, and Thailand through Puerto Rico were checked 15 November 2026. A fact is a sentence plus a URL plus a date. The date is the day we read the source, not the day the climate period ended.</p>
    <h2>URL scheme</h2>
    <p>One place, one URL. A country is <code>/mexico/</code>. A city is <code>/mexico/cancun/</code>. The same pattern is used for Canada, the United Kingdom, Italy, France, the Dominican Republic, Spain, Germany, Japan, Ireland, Portugal, the Netherlands, Greece, Iceland, Switzerland, Austria, Czechia, Croatia, Hungary, Poland, Turkey, Morocco, Egypt, Costa Rica, Jamaica, the Bahamas, Aruba, Panama, Colombia, Peru, Brazil, Argentina, Chile, Thailand, South Korea, Indonesia, and Australia. Hawaii (a US state) and Puerto Rico (a US territory) use the same pattern, so Honolulu is <code>/hawaii/honolulu/</code>. Dubai, New York, Miami, Los Angeles, and San Francisco sit under <code>/united-arab-emirates/</code> and <code>/united-states/</code>, which have no sheet of their own yet. Months stay in the table. They do not get twelve extra URLs.</p>
    <h2>Climate</h2>
    <p>Mexico uses Servicio Meteorológico Nacional normals for 1991–2020, issued 6 November 2026, one station file per place. Canada uses Environment and Climate Change Canada's 1991–2020 composite normals. The United Kingdom uses Met Office 1991–2020 location averages, except that the UK sheet is the HadUK-Grid national block printed on that page, not Heathrow. Italy, France, Spain, Germany, the Dominican Republic, Japan, Ireland, the Netherlands, Iceland, Switzerland, Austria, Czechia, and Croatia use the WMO 1991–2020 normals distributed by NOAA NCEI, one named station per sheet. Kyoto is not in that Japanese submission, so its sheet uses the Japan Meteorological Agency's own 1991–2020 normal. Portugal is not in the NCEI collection. Its sheet uses IPMA's 1991–2020 fiche and says the fiche calls the series hybrid. Greece is not in the collection either. Greece and Athens use the Hellenic National Meteorological Service table for Elliniko and print the period on that page, 1955–2010. Santorini is not on the South Aegean list of that service, so its climate cells stay blank. Hungary, Poland, Turkey, Morocco, Egypt, Costa Rica, Jamaica, the Bahamas, Colombia, Peru, Brazil, Argentina, and Chile also use the NOAA NCEI files, one named station per sheet; Lima, Rio de Janeiro, and Pudahuel have no usable file, so Peru uses Granja Kcayra, Brazil uses Brasília, and Chile uses Quinta Normal. Dubrovnik is not in the Croatian submission; its sheet prints only the DHMZ 1961–2024 precipitation column, because that page gives mean temperature rather than highs and lows. Aruba uses the Meteorological Department Aruba 1991–2020 summary, which does not name a station. Panama has no folder and no national 1991–2020 table was found, so its cells are blank. Thailand through Puerto Rico also use the NOAA NCEI files, including the United States folder for the US cities, Hawaii, and Puerto Rico. Vietnam has no folder, and both Bali files print no monthly values. A later gap fill gave both a precipitation column from the Japan Meteorological Agency's ClimatView 1991–2020 normals, which are computed from CLIMAT reports and have no normal high or low: Da Nang (48855) for Vietnam, because Hanoi has no normal there and Ho Chi Minh City has no rain normal, and Denpasar (97230) for Bali. The JMA mean temperatures are printed in each sheet's text. South Africa has no Pretoria or Johannesburg file, so it uses Cape Town International Airport. Where a month is blank in a file, the cell is blank. Where a temperature row is tagged with another station's number, or matches another station exactly while the rest of the file does not, that row is left off.</p>
    <p>We do not name a best month. The page restates the highest high, the lowest low, and the heaviest and lightest water-column months, and leaves the choice to the reader.</p>
    <h2>The other facts</h2>
    <p>Plugs and voltage are the IEC world-plugs country lines. The 15 November lines for the new countries were read from the 16 August 2026 archive of that page, because a direct download did not return it. Driving is a statute or a government page, quoted in our own sentences. Greece uses Article 20 of Law 5209/2025. Iceland uses Article 18 of the 2019 traffic act. Tipping is a tourism office, or a government consumer or labour agency, and only when that page states a practice. Ireland's line is the Ireland.com taxi page. Holland.com, Visit Greece, Switzerland Tourism, VisitCzechia, and Croatia.hr state a practice and are quoted. Austria and Iceland still have no such page on this pass. Tap water is whatever CDC's traveler page actually says. Ireland's page is more specific than the others and is quoted. The rest of the new countries, like France, Spain, Germany, and Japan, do not get a yes or a no. Currency names and codes are ISO 4217, maintained by SIX. Time zone names are IANA's. Where zone1970.tab has folded a country's traditional name into another city's zone, the sheet prints the name in zone.tab and cites the link in the backward file. For the 15 November batch from Dubrovnik to Chile, driving lines come from Hungary's KRESZ, Turkey's Law 2918, Colombia's Law 769, Brazil's Traffic Code, and Chile's Traffic Law; tipping lines come from Visit Hungary, Poland.travel, Visit Costa Rica, Bahamas.com, and Aruba.com. A gap fill the same day added driving for Poland (Road Traffic Act art. 16), Jamaica (Visit Jamaica), and the Bahamas (Bahamas.com), and tipping for Peru (PROMPERÚ), Argentina (INPROTUR), Colombia (Superintendencia de Industria y Comercio), Chile (Dirección del Trabajo), Panama (ACODECO via Panamá Digital), and Brazil (Visit Brasil). For Thailand through Puerto Rico, driving lines come from Tourism Australia, Tourism New Zealand, South African Tourism, and the Florida, California, and Hawaii statutes; tipping lines come from Tourism New Zealand and South African Tourism. US sheets have no tap-water line, because no source was opened for them; Puerto Rico has a CDC page. US entry links go to CBP's page for international visitors. Entry requirements are a link, not a matrix. Every sheet now also carries three country-level lines, shared by its city sheets and read on 15 November 2026. The travel advisory level is the U.S. Department of State's, taken from its travel advisories feed, with the date the feed gives and a link to the advisory; US states and territories have no advisory, and the sheet says so. Health and vaccines is a short summary of CDC's Travelers' Health destination page in our own words, with CDC's travel health notices and a link; it is not medical advice. Emergency numbers come from the Emergency services list in the UK Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office's travel advice (GOV.UK), except the United Kingdom (GOV.UK's Contact the police page, which gives only the police number) and Puerto Rico (the Puerto Rico Department of Public Safety's 9-1-1 bureau). The twelve month lists apply one rule to each sheet's own climate table, an average high of 20–30 °C and under 100 mm of precipitation, and say which sheets could not be rated. The drive-on-the-left list is built from the sheets' driving lines and their sources. A second gap fill on 15 November added driving for Thailand and Bangkok (TAT Newsroom), Singapore (Highway Code, R11), South Korea and Seoul (VisitKorea), India (Rules of the Road Regulations, 1989), Indonesia and Bali (Law 22 of 2009), Vietnam (Law 36/2024/QH15), Puerto Rico (Discover Puerto Rico, which says only that traffic keeps the same side as the mainland), New York (NYS DMV Driver's Manual), Peru (DS 016-2009-MTC), Panama (Executive Decree 640 of 2006), and Argentina (Ley 24.449). It added tipping for the US sheets from Brand USA's Visit The USA, for South Korea and Seoul from VisitKorea, and for Turkey and Istanbul from Go Türkiye, which covers only the esnaf lokantası. The US sheets' tap-water line is Visit The USA's sentence, not a CDC or EPA one.</p>
    <h2 id="gaps">Left off on purpose</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Toronto (City) rainfall is blank for April through October, so the sheet prints precipitation instead.</li>
      <li>Vancouver Harbour has no rainfall element. The sheet prints precipitation and does not borrow the airport.</li>
      <li>No tipping percentage for Toronto, Vancouver, Banff, or for Canada as a whole. Mexico's page says tipping is voluntary because that is what Profeco wrote, not a percent.</li>
      <li>Venice Tessera's March average high is blank in the WMO file.</li>
      <li>Roma Ciampino is missing summer precipitation, so Rome uses Fiumicino.</li>
      <li>Puerto Vallarta station 14116 has no 1991–2020 normal. The sheet uses El Cuale.</li>
      <li>Ringway, the old Manchester Airport station, closed in 2004. The sheet uses Woodford.</li>
      <li>Naples Capodichino leaves May and July temperatures, and July precipitation, blank.</li>
      <li>Punta Cana's maximum-temperature row is numbered as Santo Domingo, and its minimums match Santo Domingo's. Those temperatures are not printed. Precipitation is.</li>
      <li>Japan's IEC line lists both 50 hertz and 60 hertz and does not assign a city, so the sheet does not either.</li>
      <li>Kyoto is not in the NOAA Japan submission. Its numbers are the JMA normal, and the position is the OSCAR record.</li>
      <li>Portugal's IPMA fiche says the 1991–2020 series is hybrid, including WRF-modelled values. The numbers are printed with that caveat.</li>
      <li>Greece and Athens use Elliniko, 1955–2010, and say so. Santorini is not on the HNMS South Aegean list that was opened, so its climate cells stay blank.</li>
      <li>Zürich/Fluntern's file has no number-of-years row. The monthly values are still the ones in the file.</li>
      <li>No tourism-office tipping line was opened for Austria or Iceland.</li>
      <li>Dubrovnik prints precipitation only (DHMZ, 1961–2024). Panama has no climate table. Lima, Rio de Janeiro, and Santiago-Pudahuel are not usable in the NCEI files, so other named stations are used and the sheets say so.</li>
      <li>No official driving-side source was found for Morocco, Egypt, Costa Rica, or Aruba, and no official tipping guidance for Morocco, Egypt, or Jamaica. Turkey's tipping line covers only the esnaf lokantası.</li>
      <li>Vietnam and Bali have no climate table. South Africa uses Cape Town because the collection has no Pretoria or Johannesburg file.</li>
      <li>No official driving-side source was found for the United Arab Emirates (Dubai). No official tipping guidance was found for Australia, Sydney, Thailand, Bangkok, Vietnam, Indonesia, Bali, Singapore, India, Dubai, or Puerto Rico; the Australia, Dubai, Singapore, and Jamaica tourism pages that were opened gave no tipping text. Vietnam and Bali have rain normals but no normal highs or lows.</li>
      <li>The full list, including a Tacubaya file that disagrees with an older WMO copy, is in the repository file GAPS.md.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
